# SnapDock build configuration
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values.
# `.env` is git-ignored. Never commit real values.
#
# ALL OF THIS IS OPTIONAL. It only enables the Google Drive destination. Saving
# captures to your device works with none of it configured.
#
# Full walkthrough: docs/GOOGLE_OAUTH_SETUP.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chromium (Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OAuth 2.0 Client ID, application type "Chrome Extension".
# Read by chrome.identity.getAuthToken, which lets the browser hold the token.
#
# This value is public (it ships inside manifest.json) but is kept out of the
# repository so every developer builds against their own Google Cloud project.
# There is NO client secret. Extensions are public clients and must never
# embed one.
SNAPDOCK_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# Optional, but needed for Google sign-in during local development.
# Base64 public key that pins an unpacked Chromium extension to a fixed
# extension ID, because the OAuth client above is bound to one specific ID.
# Generate it with: npm run keygen
SNAPDOCK_EXTENSION_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Firefox
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OAuth 2.0 Client ID, application type "Web application".
#
# A SEPARATE client is required: Firefox has no getAuthToken, so SnapDock uses
# the authorization code flow with PKCE, and that needs a client whose
# authorized redirect URI is registered. Register the URL that
# identity.getRedirectURL() returns, which is derived from the add-on ID in
# browser_specific_settings.gecko.id and is therefore stable.
#
# Still no client secret: PKCE replaces it.
SNAPDOCK_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_FIREFOX=
